{#ch02-0001}
# The Sayings of Hávi [^6]

{#ch02-0002 ref="hav.1"}
::: stanza
Before advancing through all doorways,
one must look around,
one must peer around;
for one never knows for certain where enemies
are sitting on the boards.[^7]
:::

{#ch02-0003 ref="hav.2"}
::: stanza
‘Hail to the givers!’[^8] A guest has come in.[^9]
Where shall he sit?
He’s very anxious,[^10] the one who has
to test his fortune on the firewood.[^11]
:::

{#ch02-0004 ref="hav.3"}
::: stanza
Fire is required for the one who has come in
and is chilled at the knee;
food and clothes are required for the man
who has travelled over mountains.
:::

{#ch02-0005 ref="hav.4"}
::: stanza
Water is required for the one who comes to a meal,
a towel and a warm invitation,
a good disposition,[^12] if he can get it,
conversation and silence in return.[^13]
:::

{#ch02-0006 ref="hav.5"}
::: stanza
Wits are required by the one who wanders widely;
everything is easy at home;
he who knows nothing and sits among the wise
becomes the subject of winking.[^14]
:::

{#ch02-0007 ref="hav.6"}
::: stanza
One shouldn’t be boastful of one’s brains,
but rather be reserved of mind;
when a wise and reticent man comes to homesteads,
misfortune seldom befalls the wary;[^15]
for one never gets a more unfailing friend
than great common sense.[^16]
:::

{#ch02-0008 ref="hav.7"}
::: stanza
The cautious guest, when he comes to a meal,
is silent with strained hearing;
he listens with his ears and looks with his eyes;
so every wise man spies things out before himself.
:::

{#ch02-0009 ref="hav.8"}
::: stanza
Happy is that one who earns himself
praise and kindness-staves;[^17]
it’s less easy to deal with what one has to own
in the heart of another.[^18]
:::

{#ch02-0010 ref="hav.9"}
::: stanza
Happy is he who himself possesses
acclaim and intelligence while he lives;
for a man has often had bad advice
from another’s breast.
:::

{#ch02-0011 ref="hav.10"}
::: stanza
One doesn’t bear a better burden on the road
than great common sense;
better than wealth it seems in a strange place;
such is a poor man’s means of existence.[^19]
:::

{#ch02-0012 ref="hav.11"}
::: stanza
One doesn’t bear a better burden on the road
than great common sense;
one can’t carry worse provisions through the country[^20]
than overindulgence in ale.
:::

{#ch02-0013 ref="hav.12"}
::: stanza
It’s not as good as they say it’s good,
ale, for the sons of men;
for the more a man drinks,
the less he knows his mind.
:::

{#ch02-0014 ref="hav.13"}
::: stanza
It’s called the heron of oblivion,[^21] the one that stands quietly[^22] over ale-feasts;[^23]
it steals a man’s wits;[^24]
with this bird’s feathers[^25] I was fettered[^26]
in the court[^27] of Gunnlǫð.[^28]
:::

{#ch02-0015 ref="hav.14"}
::: stanza
I got drunk, got extremely drunk,
at the house of wise Fjalarr;[^29]
in that case[^30] the best ale-feast is one where each man
gets his faculties back.[^31]
:::

{#ch02-0016 ref="hav.15"}
::: stanza
A ruler’s child must be reserved and thoughtful
and brave in battle;
every man must be merry and cheerful
until he meets his death.
:::

{#ch02-0017 ref="hav.16"}
::: stanza
A foolish[^32] man thinks he will live forever,
if he bewares of[^33] battle;
but old age will give him no peace,
even if spears give it to him.
:::

{#ch02-0018 ref="hav.17"}
::: stanza
A fool stares when he comes to a friend’s house;
he mumbles to himself or stays silent;
all at once, if he gets a swig,
the man’s wits are gone.[^34]
:::

{#ch02-0019 ref="hav.18"}
::: stanza
Only the one who wanders widely
and has journeyed much
knows what disposition commands each man;
he’s knowing about the mind.
:::

{#ch02-0020 ref="hav.19"}
::: stanza
One mustn’t hog the bowl, but drink mead in moderation;[^35]
let one say something useful or be silent;
nobody will blame you for such bad manners,
if you go to bed early.
:::

{#ch02-0021 ref="hav.20"}
::: stanza
A greedy man, unless he knows his inclination,
eats himself into life-sorrow;
the stomach often brings scorn on a foolish man,
when he comes among the wise.
:::

{#ch02-0022 ref="hav.21"}
::: stanza
Herds know it, when they must go home,
and then go from the grass;[^36]
but a foolish man never knows
the measure of his maw.[^37]
:::

{#ch02-0023 ref="hav.22"}
::: stanza
A wretched man and one of wicked character[^38]
laughs at everything;
he doesn’t know what he needed to know,
that he’s not short of faults.
:::

{#ch02-0024 ref="hav.23"}
::: stanza
A foolish man stays awake through all nights
and thinks about everything;
then he’s exhausted when morning arrives,
\[and\] all his trouble is as it was.
:::

{#ch02-0025 ref="hav.24"}
::: stanza
A foolish man thinks all who laugh with him
are his friends;
he doesn’t notice, though they speak ill of him,
if he sits among the wise.
:::

{#ch02-0026 ref="hav.25"}
::: stanza
A foolish man thinks all who laugh with him
are his friends;
then he finds it \[out\] when he comes to the assembly[^39]
that he has few speakers on his behalf.
:::

{#ch02-0027 ref="hav.26"}
::: stanza
A foolish man thinks he knows it all,
if he has a safe spot for himself in a corner;[^40]
he doesn’t know this — what he must say in response,
if people put him to the test.
:::

{#ch02-0028 ref="hav.27"}
::: stanza
When a foolish man comes among men,
it’s best that he keep quiet;
no one will know that he knows nothing,
unless he talks too much;
the man who knows nothing doesn’t even know
when he talks too much.
:::

{#ch02-0029 ref="hav.28"}
::: stanza
He who can ask and also answer
is considered wise;
the sons of men can conceal nothing
of what goes around men.[^41]
:::

{#ch02-0030 ref="hav.29"}
::: stanza
He who is never silent speaks too many
staves of senselessness;[^42]
a fast-talking tongue, unless it has guards,[^43]
often invokes no good for itself.
:::

{#ch02-0031 ref="hav.30"}
::: stanza
One shouldn’t make mock of another[^44]
when one visits an acquaintance;
many \[a man\] may then seem wise if he isn’t questioned
and he manages to sit quietly with his skin dry.[^45]
:::

{#ch02-0032 ref="hav.31"}
::: stanza
A guest scornful of a \[fellow\] guest
is thought wise — \[that is,\] the one who \[in this situation\] takes flight;[^46]
the one who’s sneering at a meal can’t be sure
whether he’s scoffing among enemies.
:::

{#ch02-0033 ref="hav.32"}
::: stanza
Many men are entirely friendly[^47] towards each other,
but abuse one another at a feast;[^48]
there will always be strife among men,[^49]
guest will fall out with guest.
:::

{#ch02-0034 ref="hav.33"}
::: stanza
One should often have one’s meal early,
unless one visits an acquaintance;[^50]
\[otherwise\] one sits and craves food restlessly, acts as if famished,
and can ask about few things.
:::

{#ch02-0035 ref="hav.34"}
::: stanza
It’s a very roundabout route to a bad friend’s house,
even if he lives on your way;
but straight roads lead to a good friend,
even if he’s gone further away.[^51]
:::

{#ch02-0036 ref="hav.35"}
::: stanza
One must go, not always be
a guest in one place;
a loved man becomes loathed if he stays for long
on the floor of another.
:::

{#ch02-0037 ref="hav.36"}
::: stanza
A farmstead is better, even if it’s little;[^52]
everyone’s a free man at home;
even if one has \[only\] two goats and a rope-raftered[^53] hall,
that’s still better than begging.
:::

{#ch02-0038 ref="hav.37"}
::: stanza
A farmstead is better, even if it’s little;[^54]
everyone’s a freeman at home;
bloody is the heart of the one who must beg
for his food at every meal.
:::

{#ch02-0039 ref="hav.38"}
::: stanza
One mustn’t go a foot from
one’s weapons in the field;
for, out on the roads, a man never knows for certain
when he’ll have need of his spear.
:::

{#ch02-0040 ref="hav.39"}
::: stanza
I haven’t encountered a man so kind or generous with food
that to accept was not \[to be reckoned as having\] accepted,[^55]
or so liberal with his wealth
that a reward would be loathed, if he could receive one.
:::

{#ch02-0041 ref="hav.40"}
::: stanza
Of his wealth, when he has acquired it,
a man shouldn’t endure need;[^56]
often one saves for the loathed what’s meant for the loved;
many things turn out worse than one expects.
:::

{#ch02-0042 ref="hav.41"}
::: stanza
With weapons and apparel friends should gladden each other;
that’s most evident on themselves;[^57]
givers in return and repeat givers are friends longest,
if it lasts long enough to turn out well.
:::

{#ch02-0043 ref="hav.42"}
::: stanza
One should be a friend to one’s friend,
and repay gift with gift;
men should requite laughter with laughter,[^58]
and lying with a lie.
:::

{#ch02-0044 ref="hav.43"}
::: stanza
One should be a friend to one’s friend,
to him and to his friend;
but no one should be a friend
of his enemy’s friend.
:::

{#ch02-0045 ref="hav.44"}
::: stanza
Know that if you have a friend whom you trust well,
and you want to get good from him,
you must mingle your mind with his and exchange gifts,
go to see him often.
:::

{#ch02-0046 ref="hav.45"}
::: stanza
If you have another, whom you mistrust,
yet you want to get good from him,
you must speak fair with him but think false,
and repay lying with a lie.
:::

{#ch02-0047 ref="hav.46"}
::: stanza
That’s also the case with the one whom you mistrust
and whose disposition you suspect:
you must laugh with him and mince the truth;[^59]
there should be like repayment for gifts.
:::

{#ch02-0048 ref="hav.47"}
::: stanza
I was young once, I travelled alone,
then I was astray on the roads;[^60]
I thought myself rich when I met another;
man is man’s delight.[^61]
:::

{#ch02-0049 ref="hav.48"}
::: stanza
Generous, brave men live best,
they seldom nurse sorrow;
but a foolish man fears everything,
a niggard is always nervous about gifts.
:::

{#ch02-0050 ref="hav.49"}
::: stanza
Out in the country,[^62] I[^63] gave my clothes
to two tree-men;[^64]
they thought themselves champions[^65] when they had fine clothing;
a naked man is shamed.
:::

{#ch02-0051 ref="hav.50"}
::: stanza
The fir-tree that stands in a farmstead withers,
neither bark nor needles protect it;[^66]
so it is with the man whom no one loves:
how is he to live for long?
:::

{#ch02-0052 ref="hav.51"}
::: stanza
Love burns hotter than fire for five days
among false friends,
but then dies down when the sixth comes,
and all friendship worsens.
:::

{#ch02-0053 ref="hav.52"}
::: stanza
One shouldn’t give a man only great[^67] things,
one can often buy oneself love[^68] with little;
with half a loaf and with a tilted cup[^69]
I got myself a comrade.
:::

{#ch02-0054 ref="hav.53"}
::: stanza
Of little sands,[^70] of little seas,
little are the minds of men;[^71]
that’s why all people haven’t become equivalently wise;[^72]
each class of humankind is half\[-wise\].[^73]
:::

{#ch02-0055 ref="hav.54"}
::: stanza
Every man should be moderately wise,
let him never be too wise;
those people who know just enough
live the pleasantest lives.[^74]
:::

{#ch02-0056 ref="hav.55"}
::: stanza
Every man should be moderately wise,
let him never be too wise;
for the heart of a wise man seldom becomes happy,
if he who owns it is all-wise.
:::

{#ch02-0057 ref="hav.56"}
::: stanza
Every man should be moderately wise,
let him never be too wise;
let no one have foreknowledge of his fate,
one’s mind is \[then\] freest from sorrows.
:::

{#ch02-0058 ref="hav.57"}
::: stanza
Brand[^75] catches fire from brand, until it is burnt,
flame is kindled from flame;
man becomes wise from man by speech,
and too foolish from reticence.[^76]
:::

{#ch02-0059 ref="hav.58"}
::: stanza
He must rise early, the one who wants to have
another’s wealth or life;
a lounging wolf seldom gets the ham,
nor a sleeping man the victory.
:::

{#ch02-0060 ref="hav.59"}
::: stanza
He must rise early, the one who has few workmen,
and go to inspect his work;[^77]
many things will hinder the one who sleeps through the morning,
wealth is half in the hands of the willing.
:::

{#ch02-0061 ref="hav.60"}
::: stanza
Of dry sticks and bark for roofing,
a man knows his measure of this,
and of the wood that can suffice
for three months and for six.
:::

{#ch02-0062 ref="hav.61"}
::: stanza
Washed and fed let a man ride to the assembly,
even if he isn’t too well attired;
of his shoes and breeches let no man be ashamed,
or of his horse either, even if he hasn’t a good one.
:::

{#ch02-0063 ref="hav.62"}
::: stanza
When an eagle comes to the sea it snaps
and stretches at the ancient ocean;
so is a man who comes among many
and has few speakers on his behalf.[^78]
:::

{#ch02-0064 ref="hav.63"}
::: stanza
Every wise man who wants to be called sensible
must ask and answer;
one man should know, but not a second,
the nation will know, if there are three.
:::

{#ch02-0065 ref="hav.64"}
::: stanza
Every man wise in counsel should keep
his power in check;
he will find it \[out\], when he comes among the brave,
that no one is boldest of all.[^79]
:::

{#ch02-0066 ref="hav.65"}
::: stanza
For the words that one says to another,
one often receives repayment.[^80]
:::

{#ch02-0067 ref="hav.66"}
::: stanza
Much too early I came to many places,[^81]
and too late to some;
the ale had been drunk, \[or\] some was unbrewed,
the hated one seldom hits the mark.[^82]
:::

{#ch02-0068 ref="hav.67"}
::: stanza
Here and there I would be invited home
if I didn’t need food at meal-times;
or two hams might hang in the true friend’s house
when I had \[already\] eaten one.[^83]
:::

{#ch02-0069 ref="hav.68"}
::: stanza
Fire is best among the sons of men,
and the sight of the sun,[^84]
his health, if a man can keep it,
to live without disability.[^85]
:::

{#ch02-0070 ref="hav.69"}
::: stanza
A man isn’t entirely wretched, even if he’s in bad health:
one man is blessed in sons,
one in kinsmen, one in sufficient wealth,
one is well-\[blessed\] in deeds.
:::

{#ch02-0071 ref="hav.70"}
::: stanza
It’s better for the living than the unliving,
the live man always gets the cow;
I saw fire blaze up before a rich man,[^86]
but death was outside the door.[^87]
:::

{#ch02-0072 ref="hav.71"}
::: stanza
A limping man rides a horse, a one-handed man drives a flock,
a deaf man fights and wins;
it’s better to be blind than burnt;[^88]
no one has use for a corpse.
:::

{#ch02-0073 ref="hav.72"}
::: stanza
A son is better, even if he’s born late,
after the father has passed on[^89]
memorial stones seldom stand by the road
unless kinsman raises them for kinsman.
:::

{#ch02-0074 ref="hav.73"}
::: stanza
Two[^90] are the destroyers of one; the tongue is the head’s slayer;[^91]
I expect a hand inside every fur cloak.[^92]
:::

{#ch02-0075 ref="hav.74"}
::: stanza
Night is welcome to the one who trusts his provisions;
short are a ship’s yard-arms;[^93]
an autumn night[^94] is changeable;
a wealth of weather in five days,
and[^95] more in a month.
:::

{#ch02-0076 ref="hav.75"}
::: stanza
He who knows nothing doesn’t know it:
many a man becomes an ape[^96] through money;
one man is wealthy, another unwealthy,
one shouldn’t blame him for his misfortune.
:::

{#ch02-0077 ref="hav.76"}
::: stanza
Cattle die, kinsmen die,[^97]
one dies oneself the same;
but reputation never dies
for whoever gets himself a good one.
:::

{#ch02-0078 ref="hav.77"}
::: stanza
Cattle die, kinsmen die,
one dies oneself the same;
I know one thing that never dies:
the renown of every dead man.[^98]
:::

{#ch02-0079 ref="hav.78"}
::: stanza
Full cattle-pens I saw for Fitjungr’s sons;[^99]
now they bear a beggar’s staff;[^100]
wealth is just like the winking of an eye,
it’s the most fickle of friends.
:::

{#ch02-0080 ref="hav.79"}
::: stanza
If a foolish man gets hold of wealth
or a woman’s love,
his pride increases, but his intelligence never;
forward he goes, headlong into folly.
:::

{#ch02-0081 ref="hav.80"}
::: stanza
It’s proven then, when you enquire of the runes,[^101]
those of divine descent,
those which mighty powers[^102] made
and Fimbulþulr [^103] coloured;[^104]
he does best then if he keeps quiet.[^105]
:::

{#ch02-0082 ref="hav.81"}
::: stanza
One should praise a day in the evening, a woman[^106] when she’s been burned,[^107]
a sword when it’s been proven, a girl when she’s been given in marriage,
ice when it’s been crossed, ale when it’s been drunk.[^108]
:::

{#ch02-0083 ref="hav.82"}
::: stanza
One should fell a tree in a wind,[^109] row to sea in fine weather,[^110]
chat with a girl in the dark; many are the eyes of day;
one should look to a ship for speed, and to a shield for protection,
a sword for a blow, and a girl for kisses.
:::

{#ch02-0084 ref="hav.83"}
::: stanza
One should drink ale by the fire,[^111] and skate on ice,
buy a lean horse, and a dirty sword;[^112]
fatten a horse at home, but a hound at \[another’s\] house.
:::

{#ch02-0085 ref="hav.84"}
::: stanza
No one should trust a girl’s words,
or what a woman[^113] says,
because their hearts were shaped on a whirling wheel,[^114]
fickleness was laid in their breast.[^115]
:::

{#ch02-0086 ref="hav.85"}
::: stanza
In a breaking bow, in a burning flame,
in a gaping wolf, in a cawing crow,[^116]
in a grunting boar, in a rootless tree,
in a rising wave, in a boiling cauldron,[^117]
:::

{#ch02-0087 ref="hav.86"}
::: stanza
in a flying shaft, in a falling wave,
in one-night-old ice, in a coiled snake,[^118]
in a bride’s bed-talk or in a broken sword,
in a bear’s play or in a king’s child,
:::

{#ch02-0088 ref="hav.87"}
::: stanza
in a sick calf, in a self-willed slave,
in a seeress’s satisfying words,[^119] in a newly-felled man,
:::

{#ch02-0089 ref="hav.88"}
::: stanza
in a field sown early, let no one have faith,
nor in a son too soon —
weather rules the field, and sense the son;
each of them is at risk —
:::

{#ch02-0090 ref="hav.89"}
::: stanza
in one’s brother’s slayer, if one should meet him on the road,
in a half-burned house, in a very fast horse —
a horse is useless if it breaks one leg —
let no man become so trusting that he puts faith in all these\![^120]
:::

{#ch02-0091 ref="hav.90"}
::: stanza
Loving women,[^121] those who think deceitful thoughts,
is like driving a horse without spikes on slippery ice,
a frisky two-year-old and ill-trained,
or like tacking a rudderless ship against a raging wind,
or having, as a limping man, to lay hands on a reindeer on a thawing mountain.[^122]
:::

{#ch02-0092 ref="hav.91"}
::: stanza
I shall now speak plainly, for I know both —
men’s feelings for women are fickle;
when we speak most sweetly, we think most deceitfully —
that deludes wise minds.
:::

{#ch02-0093 ref="hav.92"}
::: stanza
He must speak sweetly and offer wealth,
the one who wants to win a woman’s love,
praise the radiant girl’s body,
he who woos wins.
:::

{#ch02-0094 ref="hav.93"}
::: stanza
No one should ever find fault
with another over love;
often ravishingly fair looks enthral a wise man,
when they don’t enthral a fool.
:::

{#ch02-0095 ref="hav.94"}
::: stanza
One should find fault with another
for nothing that happens to many a man;
that mighty desire[^123] makes dolts
out of wise sons of men.
:::

{#ch02-0096 ref="hav.95"}
::: stanza
The mind alone knows that which lives near the heart,
he[^124] is alone with his thoughts;
no sickness is worse for any wise man
than to be content with nothing.
:::

{#ch02-0097 ref="hav.96"}
::: stanza
I[^125] proved that[^126] when I sat in the reeds
and waited for my love;[^127]
the wise girl was flesh and heart to me,
yet I have her none the more for that.
:::

{#ch02-0098 ref="hav.97"}
::: stanza
I found Billingr’s daughter[^128] in bed,
sleeping, sun-white;
an earl’s delight seemed as nothing to me,
unless I could live with that body.[^129]
:::

{#ch02-0099 ref="hav.98"}
::: stanza
‘You must come again towards evening, Óðinn,
if you want to win the girl with words;
all will be undone, unless we alone
know of such shame together!’[^130]
:::

{#ch02-0100 ref="hav.99"}
::: stanza
Back I turned and thought myself in love,
from certain delight;[^131]
I thought this, that I would have
her whole heart and love-play.
:::

{#ch02-0101 ref="hav.100"}
::: stanza
Thus \[it was when\] I came next,
that the able warband was all awake;
with burning lights and raised wood,[^132]
thus was a misery-path[^133] appointed for me.
:::

{#ch02-0102 ref="hav.101"}
::: stanza
And toward morning, when I had come again,
the hall-guard[^134] was asleep;
then I found one of the good woman’s[^135] bitches
bound on the bed.
:::

{#ch02-0103 ref="hav.102"}
::: stanza
Many a good girl,[^136] if one gets to know her fully,
is fickle of mind towards men,
I proved that when I sought to seduce
the counsel-wise woman[^137] into deceit;
the wise girl attacked me with every insult,
and I had nothing of this woman.
:::

{#ch02-0104 ref="hav.103"}
::: stanza
A man happy at home and cheerful with guests
must be shrewd in his behaviour,[^138]
mindful and talkative, if he wants to be very knowledgeable;
he must often speak of good;[^139]
he who can say little is called a complete idiot:
that’s the sign of the unwise.
:::

{#ch02-0105 ref="hav.104"}
::: stanza
I visited the ancient giant,[^140] \[and\] now I have come back;
I got little by keeping quiet there;[^141]
with many words I spoke to my advancement
in Suttungr’s halls.[^142]
:::

{#ch02-0106 ref="hav.105"}
::: stanza
Gunnlǫð gave me on a golden seat
a drink of the precious mead;[^143]
I let her have a poor reward[^144]
for her true feelings,
for her troubled mind.
:::

{#ch02-0107 ref="hav.106"}
::: stanza
I made Rati’s mouth[^145] make room
and gnaw through rock;
above and below me stood the roads of giants;[^146]
that’s how I risked my head for it.[^147]
:::

{#ch02-0108 ref="hav.107"}
::: stanza
I’ve made good use of the well-bought look;[^148]
for the wise one, little is lacking;
because Óðrerir [^149] has now come up
to the rim of the sanctuary of men.[^150]
:::

{#ch02-0109 ref="hav.108"}
::: stanza
I doubt that I would have come back
from the courts of the giants,
if I hadn’t made use of[^151] Gunnlǫð, the good woman,
who laid her arm over me.[^152]
:::

{#ch02-0110 ref="hav.109"}
::: stanza
The following day,[^153] frost-giants went
to seek Hávi’s counsel[^154] in Hávi’s hall;
they asked about Bǫlverkr,[^155] whether he had come among the gods,
or whether Suttungr had slaughtered him.
:::

{#ch02-0111 ref="hav.110"}
::: stanza
I think Óðinn has sworn a ring-oath;[^156]
how can his pledges be trusted?
He cheated Suttungr of the drink,[^157]
and caused Gunnlǫð to weep.
:::

{#ch02-0112 ref="hav.111"}
::: stanza
It is time to chant from[^158] the sage’s seat,[^159]
at the spring of Urðr;[^160]
I saw and I was silent,[^161] I saw and I pondered;
I listened to the speech of men;
I heard them discuss runes,[^162] they weren’t silent with counsels,[^163]
at Hávi’s hall,
in Hávi’s hall;
I heard it said thus:[^164]
:::

{#ch02-0113 ref="hav.112"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir,[^165] that you take[^166] my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
don’t get up at night, unless you’re on watch
or looking, from inside, for a place to relieve yourself outside\!
:::

{#ch02-0114 ref="hav.113"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, that you take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
you mustn’t sleep in the embrace of a woman wise in magic,
so that she locks you in her limbs.[^167]
:::

{#ch02-0115 ref="hav.114"}
::: stanza
Thus she’ll ensure you won’t pay attention
to the assembly or a ruler’s speech;[^168]
you won’t want food or the pleasure of anyone’s fellowship,
you’ll go to sleep sorrowful.
:::

{#ch02-0116 ref="hav.115"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, that you take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
never lure another’s wife
into being your lover.[^169]
:::

{#ch02-0117 ref="hav.116"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
if you want to travel on fell[^170] or fjord,
provide yourself well with food.
:::

{#ch02-0118 ref="hav.117"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
never let a bad man
know your misfortunes,[^171]
because from a bad man you will never get
a reward for the good intention.
:::

{#ch02-0119 ref="hav.118"}
::: stanza
I saw the words of an evil woman bite a man high up;[^172]
a deceitful tongue was the death of him,
and yet the accusation was untrue.
:::

{#ch02-0120 ref="hav.119"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
know \[this, that\] if you have a friend, one whom you trust well,
go to visit him often,
because a road that no one treads becomes overgrown
with brushwood and tall grass.[^173]
:::

{#ch02-0121 ref="hav.120"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
draw a good man into a pleasing friendship with you,
and learn soothing charms[^174] while you live.
:::

{#ch02-0122 ref="hav.121"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
never be the first to sever
happy relations with your friend;
sorrow eats the heart if you can’t speak
your whole mind to someone.
:::

{#ch02-0123 ref="hav.122"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
you must never exchange words
with an unwise ape.[^175]
:::

{#ch02-0124 ref="hav.123"}
::: stanza
Because from a bad man you’ll never
get a reward for the goodwill,
but a good man can make you assured
of esteem by his praise.[^176]
:::

{#ch02-0125 ref="hav.124"}
::: stanza
Kinship[^177] is exchanged when anyone decides
to tell his whole mind \[to another\];
anything is better than for one to be unreliable,
he who says only what’s agreeable is no friend to another.
:::

{#ch02-0126 ref="hav.125"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
you mustn’t utter \[even\] three words in dispute with a worse man than you;
often the better man fails
when the worse man fights.
:::

{#ch02-0127 ref="hav.126"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
be neither a shoe-maker nor a shaft-maker,[^178]
unless you be one for yourself;
\[if\] the shoe is badly made or the shaft is crooked,
then evil will be invoked for you.[^179]
:::

{#ch02-0128 ref="hav.127"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
wherever you perceive evil, declare it as evil,
and don’t give your enemies peace.
:::

{#ch02-0129 ref="hav.128"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
never be gladdened by evil,
but let yourself be pleased with good.
:::

{#ch02-0130 ref="hav.129"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
you mustn’t look up in battle —
the sons of men become like a madman[^180] —
lest men cast a spell on you.[^181]
:::

{#ch02-0131 ref="hav.130"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
if you want to persuade a good woman[^182] into secret love with you
and get delight from her,
you must make fine promises and let them be firm:
no one loathes a good thing, if one gets it.
:::

{#ch02-0132 ref="hav.131"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
I bid you be wary, but not overwary;
be wariest with ale and with another’s wife,
and, third, that thieves don’t outwit you.
:::

{#ch02-0133 ref="hav.132"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
never treat a guest or a tramp
with scorn or derision.
:::

{#ch02-0134 ref="hav.133"}
::: stanza
Often those sitting inside are unsure
what kind of men[^183] are those who come;
no one is so good as to have no fault,
nor so bad as to be good for nothing.
:::

{#ch02-0135 ref="hav.134"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
never scoff at a grey-haired sage;
often what old men say is good;
often shrewd words come from a shrunken bag,[^184]
from the one who hangs among hides
and dangles among skins
and swings among wretches.[^185]
:::

{#ch02-0136 ref="hav.135"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
don’t bark at a guest or drive him to the gate;
provide well for a poor man.
:::

{#ch02-0137 ref="hav.136"}
::: stanza
Strong is the beam[^186] that must slide
to open up for all;
give an arm-ring, or visitors will invoke
every kind of injury upon your limbs.
:::

{#ch02-0138 ref="hav.137"}
::: stanza
I counsel you, Loddfáfnir, and you should take my counsels;
you’ll profit if you take them,
they’ll be good for you if you get them:
wherever you may drink ale, choose for yourself earth’s power,[^187]
for earth has strength against ale,[^188] and fire against illnesses,[^189]
oak against constipation,[^190] ear of corn against witchcraft,[^191]
elder(?)[^192] against household strife — for hatreds one must call on the moon[^193] —
earthworm[^194] against bite-illnesses,[^195] and runes against evil;
one must take earth for the flux.
:::

{#ch02-0139 ref="hav.138"}
::: stanza
I know that I hung on a windy tree[^196]
for all of nine nights,[^197]
wounded by a spear[^198] and given to Óðinn,
myself to myself,
on that tree of which no one knows
the kind of roots it runs from.[^199]
:::

{#ch02-0140 ref="hav.139"}
::: stanza
They blessed[^200] me with neither bread nor horn,[^201]
I peered down,[^202] I took up runes,[^203]
screaming I took them;[^204] I fell back from there.[^205]
:::

{#ch02-0141 ref="hav.140"}
::: stanza
Nine mighty songs I learnt[^206] from the famous son
of Bǫlþórr, father of Bestla,[^207]
and I got a drink of the precious mead,
poured from Óðrerir.[^208]
:::

{#ch02-0142 ref="hav.141"}
::: stanza
Then I began to produce seed and be fertile,[^209]
and to grow and feel well;
word sought word from my word,
deed sought deed from my deed.
:::

{#ch02-0143 ref="hav.142"}
::: stanza
Runes you will find and readable staves,[^210]
very great staves,
very stiff staves,
which Fimbulþulr [^211] coloured[^212]
and great powers created
and Hroptr [^213] of the ruling powers carved,[^214]
:::

{#ch02-0144 ref="hav.143"}
::: stanza
Óðinn among the Æsir,[^215] and Dáinn for the elves,[^216]
Dvalinn for the dwarves,[^217]
Ásviðr for the giants,[^218]
I carved some myself.[^219]
:::

{#ch02-0145 ref="hav.144"}
::: stanza
Do you know how one must carve?[^220] Do you know how one must read?[^221]
Do you know how one must colour?[^222] Do you know how one must test?[^223]
Do you know how one must invoke? Do you know how one must offer?[^224]
Do you know how one must send?[^225] Do you know how one must immolate?
:::

{#ch02-0146 ref="hav.145"}
::: stanza
Uninvoked is better than over-offered;[^226]
a gift always looks for repayment;
unsent[^227] is better than over-immolated;
thus Þundr [^228] carved before the history of peoples;[^229]
there he rose up, when he came back.[^230]
:::

{#ch02-0147 ref="hav.146"}
::: stanza
I know those songs that a ruler’s wife doesn’t know,
and no one’s son;
one is called ‘help’, and it will help you
against disputes and sorrows, and each and every suffering.[^231]
:::

{#ch02-0148 ref="hav.147"}
::: stanza
I know it, a second, which the sons of men need,
those who want to live as healers.[^232]
:::

{#ch02-0149 ref="hav.148"}
::: stanza
I know it, the third:[^233] if I have great need
of a fetter[^234] for my foes,
I blunt the edges[^235] of my enemies;
for them neither weapons nor staffs will bite.
:::

{#ch02-0150 ref="hav.149"}
::: stanza
I know it, the fourth: if people put
bonds on my arms and legs,[^236]
I chant so that I can walk;
the fetter springs from my legs,
and the shackle from my arms.
:::

{#ch02-0151 ref="hav.150"}
::: stanza
I know it, the fifth: if I see a shaft shot
in malice flying in battle,[^237]
it doesn’t fly so forcefully that I can’t stop it,
if I see it with my sight.[^238]
:::

{#ch02-0152 ref="hav.151"}
::: stanza
I know it, the sixth: if a warrior[^239] wounds me
on the roots of a strong[^240] tree[^241] —
so too with that man who provokes my hatred[^242] —
harm will devour him, rather than me.
:::

{#ch02-0153 ref="hav.152"}
::: stanza
I know it, the seventh: if I see a high hall[^243]
blazing around bench-mates,
it doesn’t burn so broadly that I can’t save it;
I know the incantation to chant.
:::

{#ch02-0154 ref="hav.153"}
::: stanza
I know it, the eighth, which is useful
for all to learn:
wherever hatred grows between a warrior’s sons,
I can swiftly settle it.
:::

{#ch02-0155 ref="hav.154"}
::: stanza
I know it, the ninth: if need arises for me
to save my ship when it’s afloat,
I calm the wind on the wave
and lull all the sea to sleep.
:::

{#ch02-0156 ref="hav.155"}
::: stanza
I know it, the tenth: if I see fence-riders[^244]
sporting in the air,
I bring it about that they go astray[^245]
to the home of their shapes(?),
to the home of their minds(?).[^246]
:::

{#ch02-0157 ref="hav.156"}
::: stanza
I know it, the eleventh: if I have to lead
long-standing friends into conflict,
I chant beneath the shield-rims, and they journey with strength,[^247]
safe to battle,
safe from battle,
they come safe from anywhere.
:::

{#ch02-0158 ref="hav.157"}
::: stanza
I know it, the twelfth: if I see a halter-corpse[^248]
dangling up in a tree,
I so carve and colour-in runes
that that man walks
and talks with me.
:::

{#ch02-0159 ref="hav.158"}
::: stanza
I know it, the thirteenth: if I have to throw
water on a young warrior,[^249]
he won’t fall, even if he comes into battle,[^250]
that man won’t sink before swords.
:::

{#ch02-0160 ref="hav.159"}
::: stanza
I know it, the fourteenth: if I have to count the gods
before a company of people,
of Æsir[^251] and elves I know all their attributes;
few fools can do that.
:::

{#ch02-0161 ref="hav.160"}
::: stanza
I know it, the fifteenth, which Þjóðreyrir, a dwarf,[^252]
chanted before Dellingr’s doors:[^253]
he chanted strength to the Æsir,[^254] and courage[^255] to the elves,
intellect to Hroptatýr.[^256]
:::

{#ch02-0162 ref="hav.161"}
::: stanza
I know it, the sixteenth: if I wish to have the whole heart
and sexual pleasure of the wise girl,
I turn the feelings of the white-armed woman
and I twist her whole heart.
:::

{#ch02-0163 ref="hav.162"}
::: stanza
I know it, the seventeenth, so that the girly girl
will be slow to leave me.
These songs, Loddfáfnir, you will[^257]
long be lacking,
though it would be good for you if you get them,
\[they would be\] useful if you take them,
needful if you receive them.
:::

{#ch02-0164 ref="hav.163"}
::: stanza
I know it, the eighteenth, which I never teach
to a girl or a man’s wife —
everything is better when only one knows;
it[^258] marks the end of the songs —
except to her alone who enfolds me in her arms,
or who might be my sister.[^259]
:::

{#ch02-0165 ref="hav.164"}
::: stanza
Now Hávi’s sayings have been spoken in Hávi’s hall,
most useful to the sons of men,
useless to the sons of men(?)\![^260]
Hail to the one who spoke! Hail to the one who knows\!
Let the one who learned profit\![^261]
Hail to those who listened\!
:::

[^6]: {_Hávi_} ‘High One’, an alias of Óðinn, is the weak form of the adjective {_hár_} ‘high’.

[^7]: The boards are those of a Norse hall’s wooden platform, which featured benches. A variant of this stanza is quoted in {_SnEGylf_} (2, p. 8); for another variant, see {_SnEUpp_} (5, p. 12).

[^8]: That is, to the hosts. These words are presumably spoken by a visitor to a hall, in expectation of hospitality.

[^9]: The anonymous ‘guest’ perhaps functions as both Everyman and Óðinn, who is called {_Gestr_} ‘Guest’ elsewhere in Old Norse literature{_._}

[^10]: Or ‘impatient’.

[^11]: The sense of the second half of this stanza is uncertain, but it may be that unexpected visitors had to sit on a stack of firewood near the hall’s entrance until they were granted a reception inside commensurate with their perceived importance.

[^12]: I.e., on the part of the host.

[^13]: I.e., words from the host, followed by silence in which the guest can himself speak.

[^14]: I.e., he is mocked by those who wink at each other.

[^15]: Or ‘the wary man seldom commits a culpable blunder’ (Evans, {_Hávamál_}, p. 78).

[^16]: Or ‘innate intelligence’ ({_manvit_}).

[^17]: I.e., praise and high esteem.

[^18]: The sense of the second half of this stanza is rather obscure.

[^19]: In other words, ‘a poor man’s existence depends on this’.

[^20]: Literally, ‘field’, ‘plain’.

[^21]: Or ‘forgetfulness’, ‘unawareness’, of the kind experienced the morning after a bout of heavy drinking. The literal sense of {_óminni_} is ‘un-memory’, but there is a likely pun on \*{_árminni_} ‘river-mouth, estuary’, a natural location for a hunting heron. Contrast Óðinn’s informative raven {_Muninn_}, whose name relates to {_muna_} ‘to remember’.

[^22]: Or ‘motionless’, the image being of a heron watching for fish to catch with its spear-like bill. Additionally, the heron was known proverbially for its vomiting and popularly associated with drunkenness. It may well be relevant to this stanza, and the next, that Óðinn, having taken the form of an eagle (originally a heron?), spewed and possibly defecated the mead of poetry after stealing it from the giant Suttungr, according to {_SnESkáld_} (I, G58, pp. 4–5). Conceivably, some Norse halls might have contained avian ornamentation related to the imagery in this stanza (cf. {_Grm._} 10).

[^23]: Or ‘over ales’.

[^24]: Or ‘men’s/people’s wits’. There might be an implicit contrast with how Óðinn, who lives on wine alone ({_Grm._} 19), enhanced his wits when drunk on the mead of poetry. In {_geð_} ‘wits’, there might be a pun on {_gedda_} ‘pike’.

[^25]: Possibly, Óðinn was himself in the form of a heron. This reference might also contain a wry allusion to feathers stuffing bedclothes which surround and cover the drunken victim.

[^26]: Perhaps implicitly as a {_fiskr_} ‘fish’, given this line’s {_f_}-alliteration.

[^27]: Or ‘enclosure’, here perhaps alluding to Gunnlǫð’s bedchamber and a bed with side-rails.

[^28]: I.e., the speaker—presumably Óðinn or a human who identifies with this god—became incapacitated by drink. {_Gunnlǫð_} ‘Battle Invitation’ is a giantess. For Óðinn’s seduction of her, by which he gains a drink of mead that filled him with poetic wisdom, see {_Háv._} 104–10.

[^29]: The identity of the ‘wise’ (or ‘fertile’) Fjalarr is uncertain. Perhaps he is Gunnlǫð’s father, though that role is assigned to Suttungr in {_Háv._} 104, 109–10 and {_SnESkáld_}. Elsewhere, the name {_Fjalarr_} (cf. {_fela_} ‘to conceal’) denotes the giant also known as Skrýmir, a cockerel that lives in a tree ({_Vsp._} 41), and a dwarf—perhaps significantly, according to {_SnESkáld_} (I, G57, p. 3), one of the dwarves who brewed the mead of poetry, which was later taken from them by Suttungr.

[^30]: I.e., when one gets overly drunk.

[^31]: I.e., recovers from drunkenness.

[^32]: Or ‘cowardly’.

[^33]: I.e., bewares of and avoids.

[^34]: Or ‘the man’s mind is revealed’.

[^35]: The scene seems to be a drinking-party, in which a drinking-bowl is passed from man to man.

[^36]: I.e., ‘leave the pasture’.

[^37]: I.e., stomach.

[^38]: The Old Norse line may present a relatively rare instance of {_v-_} (in {_vesall_}) alliterating with a vowel ({_i-_} in {_illa_}); cf. {_Háv._} 117.

[^39]: Or ‘he finds when the assembly comes round’. The assembly is a legal gathering.

[^40]: Or ‘in (a time of) misfortune’.

[^41]: Alternatively, ‘of what is said about a man/men/people’, or ‘of what befalls a man/men/people’, or ‘of what is current about a man/men/people’. In other words (whatever the words’ precise meaning), people gossip.

[^42]: I.e., senseless words.

[^43]: Or ‘unless guards possess it’, the guards, literally ‘holding (ones)’, being a metaphor for self-control.

[^44]: Literally, ‘one shouldn’t have another (as a subject) for winking at’.

[^45]: This line appears metaphorical, meaning ‘and if he manages to sit quietly and isn’t caught out’.

[^46]: The first half of this stanza may be corrupt. Its second line is metrically suspect, as the first syllable of a disyllabic word at the end of a ‘full line’ of {_ljóðaháttr_} is normally short.

[^47]: Or ‘loyal’.

[^48]: For this line to alliterate, an East Norse or pre-literary West Norse form of the verb {_rekask_} is required, namely \*{_vrekask_}; cf. {_Vm._} 53.

[^49]: Literally, ‘(hu)mankind’s strife, it will always be’.

[^50]: Or ‘friend’. The idea is probably that one should usually eat early, so as to be able to get on with one’s work, unless one is going to visit someone, in which case it is better to eat later, so as not be famished on arrival.

[^51]: Or perhaps ‘though he is (to be) met with further off’.

[^52]: I.e., it is better to have a small house than none at all. This line lacks alliteration in the Old Norse and may therefore be corrupt; emendation of {_lítit_} ‘little’ to {_bert_} ‘bare’ has been suggested.

[^53]: Or ‘withy-raftered’.

[^54]: This line may well be corrupt, as it lacks alliteration; see note to previous stanza.

[^55]: I.e., as Evans, {_Hávamál_}, p. 91 explains: ‘that accepting (of hospitality from him) was not (in his eyes) a gift (and therefore demanding repayment)”.

[^56]: I.e., a wealthy man should spend some of his riches, not suffer need from hoarding it.

[^57]: Or ‘on oneself’. Evans, {_Hávamál_}, p. 92 explains: ‘the reciprocally exchanged gifts which they bear on their bodies give the most manifest testimony to their mutual generosity’.

[^58]: The implication may that one should requite {_scornful_} laughter with the same, though advice to reciprocate kind-hearted laughter would also make sense.

[^59]: Literally, ‘speak around one’s thought’.

[^60]: Or perhaps ‘then I became wild in my ways’. The speaker may be Óðinn, the aged, wandering god.

[^61]: The observation ‘man is man’s delight’ is also made in the {_Icelandic Rune-Poem_}.

[^62]: Or perhaps ‘(For use) in the field’ ({_velli at_})—i.e., ‘On the battlefield’. The ‘clothes’ might then be armour.

[^63]: Probably Óðinn.

[^64]: The nature of these ‘tree-men’ is uncertain. Possibilities include scarecrows, road signs, farmstead trees used for hanging clothes out to dry, and human-shaped idols carved from wood. Then again, warriors are often described as trees in skaldic poetry. Cf. also {_Vsp._} 17, where Óðinn bestows a gift on the first man and woman, {_Askr_} ‘Ash’ and {_Embla_} ‘Little Elm(?)’.

[^65]: Or ‘warriors’, ‘upstanding men’ ({_rekkar_}).

[^66]: A fir-tree’s roots, shoots and bark are eaten by various farm animals, and its bark was used to make flour.

[^67]: I.e., large.

[^68]: Or ‘praise’.

[^69]: ‘Tilted’ either because it is only half full or because the cup-holder has tilted it to pour his friend half.

[^70]: I.e., sand-banks, stretches of sands or sea-shores.

[^71]: An obscure passage, but perhaps the sense is that people whose lives are restricted to islets have small minds because of their lack of experience of the world at large.

[^72]: There is perhaps an implicit contrast between the knowledge of stay-at-homes and people who travel far afield.

[^73]: The interpretation of the whole stanza is uncertain.

[^74]: Evans, {_Hávamál_}, p. 100 observes that ON {_vel mart_} otherwise means not ‘just enough’ (as translated here) but ‘a good many things’, and that to avoid contradicting the first half of the stanza, it may therefore be better to emend the second half to {_þeim era fyrða fegrst at lifa, er vel mart vitu_} ‘those people who know a good many things don’t live the sweetest lives’ or {_þeim er fyrða fegrst at lifa, er vel mart vitut_} ‘those people who don’t know a good many things live the sweetest lives’.

[^75]: I.e., a piece of firewood.

[^76]: Or ‘from folly’.

[^77]: Perhaps emend to {_ok ganga verka á vit_} ‘and go to inspect (his) works’ (Evans, {_Hávamál_}, p. 102).

[^78]: This man is perhaps to be imagined making his case frantically, turning his head this way and that, and snatching at every opportunity amid a ‘sea’ of opponents.

[^79]: Perhaps the lesson here is to avoid throwing one’s weight around, as one day one will face someone even bolder than oneself. Cf. {_Fm._} 17.

[^80]: If there was once a second half to this stanza, it is lost.

[^81]: The speaker is perhaps Óðinn again.

[^82]: Literally, ‘seldom hits in the joint’.

[^83]: Possibly the idea is that, just as niggardly folk often invite the speaker home when they know he will not want food, so he is often unfortunate enough to have eaten before arriving at the house of a good friend whose larder turned out to be stocked for two people.

[^84]: I.e., either ‘the appearance of the sun’ or ‘the ability to see the sun’, but the former is more likely.

[^85]: Or ‘to live without moral failing’.

[^86]: Possibly the fire of his hearth.

[^87]: Alternatively, ‘I saw fire blaze up for a rich man, but he was dead outside the door’; or ‘I saw fire blaze up to the disadvantage of a rich man \[by burning his house down?\], and he was dead outside the door.’

[^88]: I.e., cremated; cf. {_Háv._} 81.

[^89]: I.e., it is better to have a son than no son, even if he is born after his father’s death.

[^90]: I.e., two people.

[^91]: That ‘careless talk costs lives’ is a familiar idea, but in societies founded on principles of honour and shame, speech can also be used as a weapon to destroy people’s reputations—and lives.

[^92]: Literally, ‘for me there is expectation of a hand in every fur cloak’. This stanza is in {_málaháttr_}. It is often considered an interpolation, as is the next.

[^93]: A statement of uncertain significance. Evans, {_Hávamál_}, pp. 110–11 outlines three possibilities: i) because short yard-arms—needed for gusty journeys through narrow fjords—support only small sails and, therefore, only slow speeds, be sure of your provisions, as you may face many overnight stops before reaching your destination; ii) make sure you have food for a long voyage, as shipwrights invariably make yard-arms shorter than you would wish; iii) if your ship sinks, do not count on clinging to a yard-arm to save you from drowning, as they are short and may not support your weight.

[^94]: Literally, an ‘autumn-mask/covering’.

[^95]: Or ‘but’.

[^96]: I.e., a fool.

[^97]: The first line of this stanza also appears in {_Hák._} 21. Cf. line 108 of the Old English poem {_The Wanderer_}: {_Her bið feoh læne, her bið freond læne_} ‘Here property \[literally ‘cattle’\] is transitory \[literally ‘loaned’\], here a friend is transitory’.

[^98]: Or ‘the judgement about every dead man’.

[^99]: Fitjungr and his sons are otherwise unknown. They might be fabrications or the inhabitants of a large farm called {_Fitjar_} ‘Water-Meadows’ on the island of Storð in Hǫrðaland, Norway, who lost their home when Haraldr hárfagri took it. Another possibility is that {_fitjungr_} is a common noun for ‘rich man’, in which case the line would mean ‘Full cattle-pens I saw for a rich man’s sons’.

[^100]: I.e., they are now beggars.

[^101]: Or ‘That will be proven (or ‘tested’), what you enquire of (or ‘ask about’) from the runes’. What {_þat_} ‘it/that’ refers to is unclear—perhaps the results of one’s enquiry or simply the advisability of silence. Here runes are runic letters, as in {_Háv._} 142–45; the ‘runes’ that Óðinn acquires by self-sacrifice on the windy tree in {_Háv._} 139 are harder to define.

[^102]: I.e., gods.

[^103]: ‘Mighty Sage’ could be a name of Óðinn, the rune-master, as in {_Háv._} 142 (cf. {_Fimbultýr_} in {_Vsp._} 58). Alternatively, it may be a common noun. Cf. {_Hav._} 111, 134.

[^104]: Colours were used to paint runic inscriptions; cf. {_Háv._} 142, 144 and 157. Red is mentioned several times, as in {_Gðr. II_} 22.

[^105]: There are several possible interpretations, depending on whether {_þú_} ‘you’ and {_hann_} ‘he’ refer to two different people or just one: i) if you ask someone about (or ‘to interrogate’?) the runes, let that person work silently, in order to obtain the best result; ii) if you ask someone about (or ‘to interrogate’?) the runes, listen quietly to the answer, because it will be important; iii) do not waste your breath asking someone about the runes, as their secret will not be divulged; iv) if you yourself interrogate the runes(?), keep quiet while doing so. This is a difficult and metrically irregular stanza.

[^106]: Or ‘wife’.

[^107]: I.e., cremated; cf. {_Háv._} 71.

[^108]: {_Háv._} 81–83 are in {_málaháttr_}.

[^109]: Perhaps so that one knows which way the tree will fall.

[^110]: Perhaps because rowing into a wind is hard work.

[^111]: Or ‘one typically drinks ale by the fire’.

[^112]: Presumably because they will cost less but be just as good in the long run.

[^113]: Or ‘wife’.

[^114]: Perhaps a potter’s wheel, or the waxing and waning moon, which is called a {_hverfanda hvél_} ‘whirling wheel’ by the inhabitants of Hel ({_Alv._} 14). An allusion to the Wheel of Fortune is also possible. Cf. {_Sg._} 40.

[^115]: A close variant of the second half of this stanza appears in chapter 21 of the thirteenth-century {_Fóstbrœðra saga_} ‘Saga of the Sworn Brothers’.

[^116]: There is evidence that Norsemen believed crows were wise creatures whose calls were interpretable by those who knew the language of birds.

[^117]: {_Háv._} 85–87 are traditionally presented as two-and-a-half stanzas of {_málaháttr_}, but whether this is correct is debatable.

[^118]: Snakes often coil themselves in a defensive posture before striking.

[^119]: Like modern fortune-tellers, Norse seeresses were presumably more inclined to give their clients good news than bad; cf. {_Vsp._} 22.

[^120]: {_Háv._} 89–90 are in {_málaháttr_}.

[^121]: Literally ‘Thus is the love of women’.

[^122]: The image in the last line is comically absurd. Evans, {_Hávamál_}, p. 117 observes ‘the scene is plainly Norwegian, not Icelandic \... The point of the lines is that reindeer can be caught only on skis, which cannot be used in a thaw’.

[^123]: Love or lust.

[^124]: I.e., a troubled person. Alternatively, ‘it \[i.e., the mind\] is alone with its thoughts’.

[^125]: Óðinn; see {_Háv._} 98.

[^126]: If {_þat_} ‘that’ refers to the previous stanza, we should translate its final words as ‘than to find contentment with nothing’ (i.e., not to get the girl). Otherwise, the reference seems better suited to earlier stanzas about the troublesomeness of love for women. Perhaps an immediately preceding stanza has dropped out or a misplacement has occurred.

[^127]: Or perhaps ‘for the fulfilment of my desire’.

[^128]: More specifically, probably, ‘unmarried, virginal daughter’. ON {_mær_} can also mean ‘wife’ or ‘woman’, but this sense is rarer in poetry. {_Billingr_} ‘Twin’ is the name of a dwarf in {_Vsp._} {*H*} 13, but here the name may well denote a giant: cf. {_Gillingr_}, the name of a giant who plays a part in the story of the mead of poetry in {_SnESkáld_}. Possibly Billingr is the father of the Suttungr mentioned in {_Háv._} 104, 109–10. With his daughter’s radiance, compare that of the giantess Gerðr in {_FSk._}

[^129]: The story of Óðinn and Billingr’s daughter is known only from {_Háv_}.

[^130]: I.e., ‘of such a great social disgrace’. The words in this stanza are presumably spoken by Billingr’s daughter (or wife).

[^131]: The interpretation of the first half of this stanza is disputed.

[^132]: Possibly as cudgels. Alternatively perhaps, ‘with wood borne in (as torches?)’.

[^133]: Or {_vilstígr_} ‘path of desire’. Word-play seems likely.

[^134]: Or ‘household’.

[^135]: ‘Good’ appears at least partly ironic here.

[^136]: Again, ‘good’ seems rather ironic.

[^137]: Presumably Billingr’s daughter (or wife).

[^138]: Or ‘shrewd concerning himself’.

[^139]: The sense of this line is uncertain.

[^140]: The speaker is Óðinn. The giant is Suttungr (see footnote 142, below).

[^141]: An understatement; in other words, Óðinn gained a great deal by speaking up. He is probably referring to his seduction of the giantess Gunnlǫð, by which he gained the precious mead that her father, Suttungr, had denied him (see footnote 152, below).

[^142]: Suttungr (also {_Suttungi_}) is named as a giant in {_SnESkáld_}; giants are called ‘sons of Suttungr/Suttungi’ in {_Alv._} 34 and {_FSk._} 34. The only other tale about him, including a variant version of the story told in {_Háv_}. 105–10, appears in {_SnESkáld_} (I, G57–58, pp. 3–5), though a third version of the myth seems to be outlined in {_Háv._} 13–14.

[^143]: Similarly {_Háv._} 13, but contrast {_Háv._} 138–41, which give an entirely different account of how Óðinn gained the sacred mead. {_Gunnlǫð_} ‘Battle Invitation’ is a giantess, Suttungr’s daughter, and we are to understand that she serves Óðinn without her father’s consent. The gift of the mead possibly symbolizes a marriage between god and giantess.

[^144]: By abandoning her and taking all the mead ({_Háv._} 107).

[^145]: {_SnESkáld_} (I, G58, p. 4) records that {_Rati_} ‘Augur’ is an auger or gimlet; whether it truly is a proper noun is uncertain. Its ‘mouth’ must be its point.

[^146]: A kenning for ‘rocks’, where the giants live.

[^147]: I.e., for the mead. In {_SnESkáld_} Óðinn has the giant Baugi bore into a mountain, so that he can crawl inside as a snake to meet Gunnlǫð.

[^148]: The translation of this line is uncertain, as {_keyptr_} can mean ‘exchanged’ as well as ‘bought’, and there is a possibility that the correct reading is {_vél keypts_} ‘wile-bought’, ‘bought by craftiness’, not {_vel keypts_}. More problematically, the grammatical object, {_litr_}, can mean various things, and we cannot tell which applies here: i) ‘look’, ‘outward appearance’, perhaps with reference to an item of clothing or an ability that Óðinn acquired which enabled him to turn into a snake or a bird, or to masquerade as a workman called {_Bǫlverkr_} ‘Evil Doer’ or a handsome young man; ii) ‘looks’ or ‘complexion’, conceivably referring to Gunnlǫð’s beauty; iii) ‘colour’, perhaps referring to the mead.

[^149]: The name of a vat in which the mead of poetry was kept, according to {_SnESkáld_} (I, G58, p. 4). But the word’s etymological sense ‘Inspiration Stirrer’, ‘Spirit Rouser’ (cf. {_Óðinn_} and {_Óðr_}) seems more suited to the mead itself, and this might be the meaning here (the reference in {_Háv._} 140 is ambiguous).

[^150]: Possibly the fence surrounding Miðgarðr or Ásgarðr, or perhaps the horizon (effectively the same thing), but the interpretation of this emended line is uncertain.

[^151]: Or ‘enjoyed the help of’.

[^152]: Or ‘over whom I laid my arm’.

[^153]: Or perhaps ‘the day after the wedding’ (of Óðinn and Gunnlǫð?).

[^154]: Or ‘to ask about Hávi’s situation’.

[^155]: According to {_SnESkáld_} (I, G58, p. 4), Bǫlverkr is the name Óðinn used when obtaining the mead of poetry. But whether it is a proper noun here is uncertain: the frost-giants may simply be asking after the ‘evil-doer’.

[^156]: The identity of the speaker is uncertain. Perhaps it is the poet, or maybe it is the leader of the frost-giants upon learning—or deducing—that Óðinn was the thief. Alternatively, again, Óðinn might be sarcastically voicing the words of his detractors. Oaths sworn on rings are well-attested in Old Norse literature (cf. e.g., {_Akv._} 30).

[^157]: The sacred mead.

[^158]: Literally, ‘on’ or ‘at’.

[^159]: The sage might well be Óðinn, who is probably the {_Fimbulþulr_} ‘Mighty Sage’ of {_Háv._} 80, 142 (cf. 134). According to {_SnEGylf_} (15, p. 17), the gods’ place of judgement is by Urðr’s spring.

[^160]: One of the Nornir. Her spring or well is beneath the world-tree; see {_Vsp._} 19–20 and cf. {_Gðr. II_} 21.

[^161]: Again, the identity of the speaker is uncertain.

[^162]: Here, whether {_rúnar_} denotes ‘runes (i.e., runic letters)’, ‘secrets’ or ‘messages written in runes’ (or all of these) is unclear. ‘Runes’ are mentioned only fleetingly in the following section (in {_Háv._} 137), but become the focus of attention in {_Háv._} 142–45.

[^163]: Or ‘readings’.

[^164]: This impressive but obscure stanza evidently begins a new section (its first letter is ornate and enlarged in {*R*}), which was perhaps originally a separate poem.

[^165]: This character is otherwise unknown. His name is also obscure: {_lodd-_} might be related to {_loddari_} ‘juggler’, ‘jester’, ‘tramp’ (cf. OE {_loddere_} ‘beggar’), {_loddi_} ‘shaggy dog’ and {_loðinn_} ‘shaggy’; {_fáfnir_} ‘embracer’ is otherwise the name of the treasure-hoarding dragon of the Sigurðr-poems (see {_Fm._}); cf. note to {_Háv._} 164. Stt. 111–37 are often known as {_Loddfáfnismál_} ‘Words for Loddfáfnir’.

[^166]: Or ‘learn’, ‘accept’, ‘ingest’. Cf. the ambiguous use of {_nema_} in {_Háv._} 139.

[^167]: Or ‘so that she locks you in your limbs’, i.e., leaves you unable to move, perhaps in a state of paralysis.

[^168]: Or ‘a ruler’s business’.

[^169]: Cf\. {_Vsp._} 38.

[^170]: I.e., mountain.

[^171]: The Old Norse line may present a relatively rare instance of {_v-_} (in {_vita_}) alliterating with a vowel ({_ó-_} in {_óhǫpp_}), even though that vowel would normally be unstressed; cf. {_Háv._} 22.

[^172]: I.e., in the head (possibly).

[^173]: Cf\. {_Grm._} 17.

[^174]: It is unclear what is meant by the unique compound {_líknagaldr_}. ON {_galdr_} (here pl.) normally denotes a ‘sung spell’, ‘incantation’ (cf. OE {_g(e)aldor_}), and {_líkna-_} is related to {_líkn_} ‘help’, ‘benevolence’, so the etymological meaning is ‘helpful spells’ or ‘kind incantations’; cf. the {_ljóð_} ‘magical songs’ mentioned later in the poem. But perhaps the intended sense is merely ‘the art of making yourself loved (by being charming?)’; cf. {_líknstafi_} (acc. pl., ‘kindness-staves’, i.e., high esteem) in {_Háv._} 8.

[^175]: I.e., oaf.

[^176]: The interpretation of the last line is somewhat uncertain.

[^177]: {_Sif_} ‘kinship’ is here used metaphorically.

[^178]: Specifically, a maker of spear-shafts.

[^179]: I.e., the owner will curse you.

[^180]: ON {_gjalti_} (dat. sg.) ‘madman’ is a borrowing of Old Irish {_geilt_}, and the belief that warriors who looked up in battle were liable to be driven mad is probably of Celtic origin. The most famous instance appears in a medieval Irish story about a man called Suibhne who looked up during a battle and was promptly transformed into a bird-like creature in fulfilment of a saint’s curse. Similarly, medieval Welsh tradition records that Merlin saw a terrible monster when he looked up during a battle, the sight of which drove him mad. Again, Scottish tradition tells of a wildman called Lailoken who went mad in a battle when he saw bands of warriors in the sky and heard a voice claiming he was responsible for the carnage.

[^181]: These ‘men’ ({_halir_}) are mysterious, but, judging from the tale of Lailoken (mentioned in footnote 180, above), they may be supernatural sky-warriors. Another puzzle is presented by {*R*}’s possessive pronoun {_þitt_} ‘your’, which ought to qualify a neut. noun (such as {_andlit_} ‘face’ or {_líf_} ‘life’, perhaps), which is lacking; it is therefore emended {_þik_} ‘you’.

[^182]: Here ‘good’ appears somewhat ironic, though the woman might be good-looking or of good family.

[^183]: Or perhaps ‘of whose kin’.

[^184]: Apparently an unflattering description of an old man.

[^185]: The significance of the last three lines is obscure, but the image might be of an old man (Óðinn?) hanging in a tree among the skins of sacrificed beasts and humans. Óðinn, the old god and sage, hangs himself from a windy tree in search of wisdom in {_Háv._} 138–39.

[^186]: A door-bar.

[^187]: Earth was thought to have magical properties; cf. e.g., {_Hdl._} 38 and 43 and an Old English metrical charm against {_wæterælfadl_} ‘water-elf disease’ whose speaker must repeat the words {_Eorþe þe onbere eallum hire mihtum and mægenum_} ‘May earth carry you away with all her mights and mains’; see also note to {_Gðr. II_} 21.

[^188]: Perhaps ale adulterated by bearded darnel.

[^189]: Whether through cautery or the use of flames and smoke to combat evil spirits.

[^190]: Or tenesmus, often a symptom of dysentery.

[^191]: Ears of corn were used in Scandinavian folk-medicine to ward off malevolent creatures.

[^192]: Conjectural\. Possibly the reference is to a ‘household tree’, in which lived spirits that protected the home from strife and illness. Alternatively, perhaps read {_hǫll_} ‘hall’.

[^193]: This instruction is obscure.

[^194]: ON {_beiti_} can also mean ‘pasturage’. Other meanings that have been proposed are ‘beet’ and ‘alum’.

[^195]: This use of earthworms (if present here) has precedents in classical antiquity and parallels in Old English medical texts; see Evans, {_Hávamál_}, pp. 133–34.

[^196]: The speaker is Óðinn. The tree is probably Yggdrasill, the world-tree which served as his gallows. In {_Hál._} 5 {_vingameiðr_} ‘swaying \[or perhaps ‘windy’\] tree’ similarly refers to a tree that serves as a gallows.

[^197]: That is, nine days and nights—the Germanic peoples measured time by nights, rather than by days. A period of ‘nine nights’ was often associated with suffering followed by transformation or fulfillment; cf. {_Grm._} 2, {_FSk._} 39, 41.

[^198]: The spear is Óðinn’s special weapon. According to chapter 9 of {_Ynglinga saga_}, Óðinn had himself marked with a spearpoint on his death-bed.

[^199]: Or, less likely, ‘from whose roots it runs’. Possibly {_hvers_} should be emended to {_hverjum_}, to give the sense ‘from what roots it runs’. Cf. {_Fj._} 20, {_Grm._} 31. Scholars often refer to {_Háv._} 138–45 as {_Rúnatal_} ‘The Rune-List’.

[^200]: Or ‘refreshed’. Who ‘they’ are is unspecified.

[^201]: I.e., a drinking-horn.

[^202]: The semantic range of the verb {_nýsa_} includes ‘peer’, ‘look’, ‘pry, ‘search’, ‘enquire’ and ‘investigate’, so there could be an intellectual or spiritual dimension to this act; perhaps Óðinn is not just looking down physically but also scouring the depths of his unconscious or of Hel, the subterranean land of the dead.

[^203]: ON {_rún_} can denote a ‘letter of the runic alphabet’, an ‘inscription written in runes’, a ‘mystery’ and a ‘secret’. Any or all of these senses may be present here, as {_nema_} ({_upp_}) ‘to take (up)’ could refer to either the lifting of physical objects or the acquisition of abstract knowledge.

[^204]: Or, perhaps, ‘I learnt \[literally ‘picked up’\] secrets, screaming I learnt them’.

[^205]: Or, just possibly, ‘I fell again from there’, but it seems unlikely that Óðinn would undergo this ordeal of discovery more than once. {_Háv._} 145 may describe Óðinn’s subsequent resurrection. Some editors present {_Háv._} 139 as {_ljóðaháttr_}, but the alliteration of the second full line is then somewhat suspect.

[^206]: Or ‘took’.

[^207]: Taken by itself, the text is ambiguous: it could mean either ‘from the famous son of Bǫlþórr, \[from the son who was also the\] father of Bestla’ or ‘from the famous son of Bǫlþórr, \[Bǫlþórr being the\] father of Bestla’. But {_SnEGylf_} records that Bestla, Óðinn’s mother, was the daughter of the giant Bǫlþorn, who is presumably the person here called Bǫlþórr. The identity of Bǫlþórr’s famous son is uncertain—though the giant Mímir has been suggested—but it is significant that he is apparently Óðinn’s maternal uncle, as the uncle-nephew relationship was important in early Germanic societies.

[^208]: Or ‘and I got a drink of the precious mead, \[having been\] sprinkled with Óðrerir’. It is, therefore, unclear whether Óðrerir is here the name of the container in which the mead was kept, or of the mead itself.

[^209]: Or ‘wise’. Both senses may be operative.

[^210]: Or ‘interpretable/meaningful staves’. The reference is to runic letters.

[^211]: Probably Óðinn; cf. {_Háv._} 80 and 111.

[^212]: Or perhaps ‘carved’; cf. {_Háv._} 80.

[^213]: An alias of Óðinn.

[^214]: Or ‘and Hroptr carved among the ruling powers’. Óðinn has already referred to himself in the third person in {_Háv._} 138, and may do so again here, and at the start of the next stanza, and in {_Háv._} 145.

[^215]: Or ‘the gods’.

[^216]: {_Dáinn_} ‘Dead One’ is a dwarf in {_Vsp._} {*H*} 11 and 13 and {_Hdl._} 7, but dwarves and elves seem to some extent interchangeable. Dáinn, like Dvalinn, is also the name of one of four stags which gnaw the shoots of the world-tree in {_Grm._} 33.

[^217]: {_Dvalinn_} ‘Delayed One’ is a dwarf; see {_Vsp._} 11. Dvalinn, like Dáinn, is also the name of one of four stags which gnaw the shoots of the world-tree in {_Grm._} 33.

[^218]: {_Ásviðr_} ‘God/Beam Wood(?)’ or ‘Extremely Wise’ is otherwise unknown.

[^219]: Perhaps Óðinn again; if not, the speaker is unknown.

[^220]: Alternatively, ‘Know how one must carve! Know how one must read!’, etc. Runic inscriptions are the object of at least the first three statements in this stanza.

[^221]: Or ‘interpret’.

[^222]: Cf\. {_Háv._} 80.

[^223]: How one was to ‘test’ or ‘make trial of’ runes—assuming they are the topic in question—is unclear.

[^224]: I.e., in sacrifice.

[^225]: Here {_senda_} seems to mean ‘send (in relation to sacrifice)’, as in {_Háv._} 145 {_ósent_} ‘unsacrificed’, but the precise sense is elusive.

[^226]: I.e., over-sacrificed.

[^227]: I.e., unsacrificed.

[^228]: An alias of Óðinn, possibly meaning ‘Thunder(er)’.

[^229]: Alternatively, ‘before the origin of peoples’, ‘before the destiny of humankind’ or ‘before the doom of nations’.

[^230]: Or ‘(in the place) where he rose up, when he came back.’ Either way, the meaning is obscure. It might, however, describe Óðinn’s resurrection, after he ‘fell back from there \[i.e., from the windy tree\]’ in {_Háv._} 139, in an echo that forms a concluding frame to the {_Rúnatal_} section; it would make sense for Óðinn to carve runes as soon as he returned from the deathly ordeal during which he had learnt them.

[^231]: Scholars often call {_Háv._} 146–63 the {_Ljóðatal_} ‘List of Songs’. Snorri Sturluson probably knew these verses and understood their {_ek_} ‘I’ as Óðinn, as he seems to draw on them for his description of Óðinn’s skills in {_Ynglinga saga_}.

[^232]: If there was once a second half of this stanza, it is lost.

[^233]: Literally, ‘I know it/that (as) the third’.

[^234]: Here the ‘fetter’ is presumably metaphorical.

[^235]: I.e., sword-edges.

[^236]: Alternatively, {_boglimum_}, with the same meaning, ‘limbs’, ‘arms and legs’.

[^237]: Or ‘into a host’.

[^238]: I.e., ‘with my eyes’.

[^239]: Or ‘thane’, ‘man’.

[^240]: If, instead of the adopting the emendation {_ráms_} ‘strong’, the manuscript reading {_rás_} is emended to {_hrás_}, the sense would be ‘sappy’, ‘young’, but the line would lack alliteration.

[^241]: Presumably by carving runes on them. The preposition {_á_} might have instrumental force here: ‘{_by using_} the roots’ (an interpretation that raises the possibility of poisoning). Alternatively, {_á_} may reflect a belief that the act of carving a spell in harmful runes on roots was indistinguishable from the concomitant wounding of the spell’s victim; hence the injury could be said to occur ‘{_on_} the roots’.

[^242]: Literally ‘hatreds’.

[^243]: This line’s alliteration ({_sjaunda … sé_}) appears faulty, as the verb {_sé_} ought not to take precedence over adjectives and nouns unless displaced from its normal syntactic position.

[^244]: Or ‘home-meadow riders’. This refers to evil women who were believed to sit astride fences or gates at night, or to ride over the home meadows of farms.

[^245]: Whereas the {_túnriður_} are female, in {_þeir villir fara_} ‘they go astray/wild’ the first two words are grammatically masc. (fem. would be \*{_þær villar fara_}). If not a mistake, this might indicate that their straying involved an actual or, if they are mentally confused, self-perceived change of gender.

[^246]: The interpretation of the second part of this stanza is disputed, but it ‘clearly refers to the well-evidenced Norse belief that a person’s soul ({_hugr_}) could in certain circumstances depart temporarily from his body and range abroad by itself …, sometimes taking on a new physical shape ({_hamr_}), while the owner’s body lay in a trance’ (Evans, {_Hávamál_}, p. 140). It may be that Óðinn knows a shamanistic spell which causes the females’ external, flying souls to get lost when they try to return to their normal bodies and states of mind, and which, by preventing or greatly delaying this return, destroys these women.

[^247]: Or they journey ‘triumphantly’.

[^248]: I.e., the corpse of a man hanged with a halter round his neck.

[^249]: Or ‘thane’, ‘man’. Comparably, perhaps, new-born babies are splashed with water in several Icelandic sagas in a kind of heathen rite similar in this respect to Christian baptism.

[^250]: Or ‘comes into (i.e., joins) an army’.

[^251]: Or ‘gods’.

[^252]: {_Þjóðreyrir_} may mean ‘One of the Great Stone-Heap’ or ‘Great One of the Stone Heap’; he is otherwise unknown. If this Old Norse line contains alliteration, it is of an unorthodox type.

[^253]: In Old Norse literature, dwarves are several times imagined standing by stony doors (cf. {_Vsp._} 50). {_SnEGylf_} (10, p. 13), following {_Vm._} 25, identifies {_Dellingr_} ‘Descendant of Brightness’ as the father of {_Dagr_} ‘Day’ and of the kindred of the Æsir. {_Dellingr_} also appears amid a list of apparently dwarven builders in {_Fj._} 34.

[^254]: Or ‘gods’.

[^255]: Or ‘advancement’, ‘profit’, ‘ability’, ‘prowess’.

[^256]: An alias of Óðinn in which {_-týr_} means ‘god’; the meaning of {_Hropta-_} (like the Óðinn-alias {_Hroptr_}) is uncertain; perhaps ‘God of Hidden Things’.

[^257]: This line and the rest of this stanza are arguably misplaced, having originally come after st. 163. If so, the bulk of st. 162 has been lost.

[^258]: I.e., the eighteenth song.

[^259]: As far as we know, Óðinn had no sister.

[^260]: A taunting, enigmatic conclusion, which might reflect the fact that, although useful but largely mundane advice is given in {_Háv._} 1–145, Óðinn’s magical knowledge in {_Háv._} 146–63 is tantalizingly withheld from—and therefore useless to—Loddfáfnir and the ‘sons of men’ (i.e., humans). In {*R*}, a later hand has inserted a marginal correction of the second instance of {_ýta_} ‘of men/humans’ to {_jǫtna_} ‘of giants’, a change adopted by many subsequent editors; this emendation might, however, have been mistakenly prompted by the appearance, in the outer margin of the facing leaf in {*R*} (fol. 8r), of the same word, {_jǫtna_}, as a justified emendation of {_alda_} ‘of men’ in {_Vm._} 16, perhaps encouraged by {_Háv._}’s account of how the giants lost the mead of poetry to Óðinn. Then again, if {_jǫtna_} is accepted in {_Háv._} 164, it might allude to Loddfáfnir’s potential association with the man-turned-dragon Fáfnir, who is called a {_jǫtunn_} ‘giant’ in {_Fm._} 29.

[^261]: Cf\. Hár’s parting words to Gylfi in {_SnEGylf_} (53, p. 54): {_‘Ok njóttu nú sem þú namt’_} “‘And now profit from that which \[or ‘to the extent that’?\] you have learnt!’”

